{"qid": "683a58c9a7e7fe4e7695848b", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T231601950433+1000.i_m_currently_working_on.output.json", "system": "You are the final claim-completion editor for a cited research draft. You\nreceive numbered draft lines and at most four material findings, each paired\nwith citation-local source excerpts. Return JSON only:\n{\"patches\":[{\"finding\":1,\"mode\":\"replace\",\"line\":12,\"sentence\":\"One complete sentence. [unit_id]\"}]}\n\nFor each finding, either replace one related draft line or insert one sentence\nafter a related line, or add citation markers without changing its prose. Use\n`mode` equal to `replace`, `insert_after`, or `cite`.\n\nRules:\n- Address only the numbered findings using only their ALLOWED CITATION ids.\n- Prefer `replace` when a draft line already begins the point but lacks its\n  exact name, value, comparison, population, time/scope, source, or limitation.\n- A replacement must preserve the original line's point and citations while\n  completing it; an insertion must add one genuinely absent atomic point.\n- Use `cite` for an otherwise sound uncited medical, safety, or causal\n  synthesis. Its sentence words must remain exactly unchanged; append only\n  directly supporting allowed citations.\n- For a requested measurement, state its value, population/sample, time/scope,\n  and source or study name when the excerpts support them. A nearby generic\n  study is not a substitute for the finding's subject-outcome combination.\n- For a named safety, legal, or penalty obligation, state the exact name and\n  how it constrains this request rather than offering generic caution.\n- Every sentence must be one line, at most 55 words, and end with at least one\n  allowed citation. Use each finding at most once and return at most four\n  patches.\n- Do not add headings, lists, sections, commentary, or an entire worked\n  example. Do not rewrite, delete, merge, split, or reorder any other line.\n- Omit a finding when its evidence does not directly support a compact patch.\n  An empty patches array is successful.\n", "packet": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.\n\nNUMBERED ACCEPTED DRAFT — PRESERVE UNPATCHED LINES\n1: Your professor probably means: turn free-text clues in de-identified Electronic Health Records, or EHRs, into standardized Asian-subgroup variables matching a specified CDC WONDER classification, then compare the proportion of eligible patients meeting validated autoimmune-disease definitions across those groups. \n2: An EHR is a computerized longitudinal record containing structured fields such as demographics, diagnosis codes, medications and laboratory results, plus unstructured narrative notes; NLP converts relevant narrative into analyzable fields. [shard_03549_2575] [shard_03193_44799]\n3: Here “abstract” means extract and normalize varied expressions to controlled labels, while autoimmune diseases are conditions in which immunity attacks healthy cells, including rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus. [shard_01866_16930]\n4: Treat race, ethnicity, ancestry and national origin as related but noninterchangeable social variables, retain each label’s wording and source, and never interpret an administrative category as biological ancestry. [shard_03064_71833]\n5: A synthetic structured record might read “patient_id=P1042; race=Asian; ethnicity=Not Hispanic; detailed_race=Filipino; language=Tagalog,” where “Filipino” is direct subgroup evidence but “Tagalog” alone is not. \n6: A synthetic note might say, “Patient self-identifies as Filipino American; rheumatology follows her for systemic lupus,” making “Filipino American” and “systemic lupus” the exact spans to extract. \n7: For P1042, the pipeline would normalize “Filipino American” to Filipino, combine lupus text with structured diagnostic and clinical evidence, merge duplicate encounters to one patient, and count that patient once in the Filipino lupus numerator. \n8: Before NLP, audit whether de-identification preserved detailed demographics, original note types, usable dates, ICD codes, medications, laboratories, patient and encounter identifiers, and variables defining active care. \n9: CDC WONDER is a collection of separately documented query databases rather than one dataset, so the professor must identify the intended database, release and codebook. [shard_05750_57588]\n10: If she means vital-statistics mortality data, the historically prominent death-certificate subgroups are Asian Indian, Chinese, Filipino, Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese, while newer single-race tabulations can include Other Asian and expose as many as 31 race categories. [shard_00350_78120] [shard_03064_71833] [shard_04312_21837]\n11: The Multiple Cause of Death database uses ICD-10-coded underlying and contributing causes, covers national through county residence, and supplies denominators for crude and age-adjusted mortality rates, not autoimmune prevalence. [shard_04026_74031] [shard_01519_72088]\n12: Single-race results begin in 2018, bridged-race results end in 2020, and those series should not be joined as though their classifications were unchanged. [shard_04312_21837]\n13: Counts below ten are generally suppressed, detailed county denominators can prevent age-adjusted estimates, and exports should retain query notes because automated access and reproducibility are constrained. [shard_03758_50956] [shard_03567_55288] [shard_00293_53563]\n14: Begin NLP with an interpretable dictionary of approved labels, spelling variants and abbreviations, plus regular expressions for self-identification, negation, family references and historical mentions. \n15: Clinical named-entity recognition, or NER, marks subgroup mentions and contextual attributes, while normalization maps expressions such as “Filipino-American” to the agreed CDC category. [shard_02454_21127]\n16: A supervised classifier can exploit broader context but requires manually labeled examples, whereas an LLM may reduce initial engineering yet introduces privacy, hallucination and reproducibility risks and must remain in an approved secure environment. [shard_03468_57413] [shard_02484_70922] [shard_04699_27397]\n17: Preserve multiracial identities as multiple labels or a prespecified category, distinguish explicit self-report from staff-entered or inferred evidence, and assign unknown when evidence is absent. \n18: Do not classify from names, language or geography without explicit approval and validation because these proxies overlap and can misclassify people. [shard_03655_15858] [shard_05619_19173]\n19: Prevalence is the proportion already affected: point prevalence uses cases present on one index date over everyone eligible then, period prevalence uses anyone affected during an interval over that interval’s average eligible population, and incidence instead counts new onsets among people initially disease-free. [shard_04604_9387] [shard_04300_84874]\n20: A defensible one-year denominator might be unique adults with at least one encounter during 2025 and sufficient prior observation, stratified by extracted subgroup, rather than every historical chart. \n21: For lupus, pilot repeated M32 diagnoses or rheumatologist documentation plus supporting antimalarial, ANA, dsDNA or complement evidence; for rheumatoid arthritis, pilot repeated M05/M06 diagnoses plus rheumatology text, disease-modifying medication or rheumatoid-factor evidence. [shard_06487_58913] [shard_06123_32469] [shard_02084_76011]\n22: If 12 distinct Filipino patients meet the lupus phenotype among 600 eligible Filipino patients, period prevalence is 12/600=0.020, or 2.0%, reported with a binomial 95% confidence interval and suppressed under the approved small-cell rule when necessary. \n23: Estimate crude and age/sex-standardized prevalence, never substitute WONDER’s national mortality denominator for the health system’s EHR denominator, and state that results describe the care population because EHR inclusion depends on health-care use. [shard_01425_9658]\n24: A large English EHR study measured rheumatoid-arthritis incidence and point prevalence, but I could not establish a published autoimmune-prevalence study jointly covering the detailed U.S. Asian categories above. [shard_02084_76011]\n25: Write an annotation manual covering allowable evidence, normalization, multiracial cases, uncertainty, negation, relatives and missingness, then have two reviewers independently annotate a stratified sample, calculate agreement, adjudicate disagreements and freeze a gold standard. [shard_06007_6716] [shard_02132_74384]\n26: Split by patient, not note, into training, validation and untouched test sets, and report precision, recall and F1 overall and within every subgroup because accuracy can conceal minority-class failures. [shard_02858_24324]\n27: Validate three separate links: subgroup extraction against human labels, autoimmune phenotypes against specialist chart review, and prevalence against denominator eligibility and deduplication. \n28: Run sensitivity analyses for missing subgroup data, uncertain mappings, phenotype strictness and plausible misclassification. \n29: Obtain the institution’s IRB or human-subjects determination and privacy approval even for de-identified records, documenting whether HIPAA Safe Harbor or Expert Determination applies, maintaining least-privilege access, suppressing small cells and prohibiting re-identification. [shard_02489_8521] [shard_03621_76660]\n30: Interpret differences as potentially reflecting documentation, access, selection, coding and institutional geography—not biological causation—and avoid treating Asian populations as homogeneous. [shard_06100_7595]\n31: Use weeks 1–2 for governance, the data dictionary and a professor-approved WONDER crosswalk; weeks 3–4 for SQL extraction and an annotation pilot; weeks 5–7 for rules or NER development; weeks 8–9 for test-set and chart-review validation; and weeks 10–12 for prevalence estimates, sensitivity analyses and reporting. \n32: Brush up on SQL, Python, pandas, regular expressions, ICD-10-CM, clinical terminology, NER and classification, confusion-matrix metrics, prevalence denominators, confidence intervals and age standardization. \n33: A practical tool stack is PostgreSQL for querying, Label Studio for annotation, cTAKES or CLAMP for clinical NLP, and R for prevalence analysis. [shard_01697_66283] [shard_02454_21127]\n34: Ask the professor which WONDER database, years and subgroup labels she means; whether only explicit self-identification is allowed; which notes and structured fields exist; which diseases, phenotype definitions and observation window she wants; how active patients are defined; and what validation sample, suppression threshold and deliverables are expected. \n\nMATERIAL FINDINGS WITH CITATION-LOCAL EVIDENCE\nFINDING 1\nREQUIREMENT: Give the closest prior measured autoimmune-disease context with population, sample size, method, quantitative result, and limitations, while stating the detailed-Asian-subgroup scope mismatch.\nDRAFT GAP: The unnamed English rheumatoid-arthritis study is described only as large and as measuring incidence and prevalence; it lacks sample size, method, quantitative result, limitations, and a concrete comparison with the intended detailed Asian subgroups.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_00350_78120_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: \"The Asian American population is very diverse in how the subgroups experience health outcomes,\" said lead study author Nilay S.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: In a large, retrospective study covering data from the last two decades, death rates for cardiovascular diseases in the U.S. varied among people from various Asian ethnicity subgroups, with death rate trends that stagnated in some subgroups and increased in others, according to new research published today in Circulation: Cardiovascular Quality and Outcomes, a peer-reviewed journal of the American Heart Association.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_04604_9387_p19]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Paradoxically, there are some point prevalences that can be accurately measured only by a longitudinal study.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Other methods may be needed if the change in population size during the period was large and did not occur at an approximately even pace.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_06100_7595_p2]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: However, while many national surveys and registries are an important source for population data, prior to 2003, they did not collect data by racial subgroups, thus aggregating the data .\nSOURCE EXCERPT: For example, in order to calculate incidence and prevalence of disease, it is important to have population-level data.\n\nFINDING 2\nREQUIREMENT: Present the guide in seven distinct sections covering interpretation, EHR examples, CDC targets, NLP, prevalence, validation/ethics, and skills/timeline.\nDRAFT GAP: The content is an unsectioned narrative rather than the explicitly required seven-section deliverable.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_04604_9387_p19]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: It is usually more helpful to know the point prevalence at the beginning of the period and the incidence rate of new cases during the period, rather than the period prevalence.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: A measure expressing the frequency of a finding in an autopsy study is a point prevalence that refers to the time of death.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_03655_15858_p3]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: NYC H+H classifies Hispanic ethnicity as a unique race category rather than an additional ethnicity.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: NYC H+H's electronic health record (EHR) database was used to identify all patients who received a SARS-CoV-2 test at NYC H+H between March 1 and May 31, with follow up through August 15, 2020.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_04604_9387_p18]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: The convenient and commonly used term ‘prevalence rate’ is disparaged by many experts who prefer to confine the term ‘rate’ to measures of the rapidity of change, and therefore use ‘prevalence’ or ‘prevalence proportion’ rather than ‘prevalence rate’, claiming that the latter is an impossible concept (Elandt-Johnson 1975).\nSOURCE EXCERPT: There are a number of prevalence measures.\n\nFINDING 3\nREQUIREMENT: Define numerator, denominator, confidence interval, validation, precision, recall, and F1 for a novice audience.\nDRAFT GAP: These terms are used or listed but not plainly defined; in particular, no explanation distinguishes precision, recall, and F1 or explains what a confidence interval and validation mean.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_02858_24324_p10]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: In this case, it’s better to look at precision and recall, and F1 score.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: F1 score: this metric considers both precision and recall results, and provides an idea of how well your text classifier is working.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_01004_15456_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: What is RA in medical terms?\nSOURCE EXCERPT: What does diagnosis code M05 79 mean?\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_04604_9387_p19]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Period prevalence refers to prevalence not at a single point in time but during a defined period (usually a specific year).\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Like other measures, point prevalence may express the findings in a specific subgroup of the population; when so used, the numerator and denominator must both refer to the same population category.\n\nFINDING 4\nREQUIREMENT: Specify executable disease-phenotype and denominator logic for both lupus and rheumatoid arthritis, including exact thresholds, logical combinations, observation window, and eligibility rules.\nDRAFT GAP: The phenotypes use indeterminate terms such as “repeated,” “plus supporting,” and “sufficient prior observation,” without diagnosis-count thresholds, explicit AND/OR logic, or a defined prior-observation requirement.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_00326_34840_p36]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: said rheumatoid arthritis.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: rheumatoid factor positive,\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_01004_15456_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Why is rheumatoid arthritis autoimmune?\nSOURCE EXCERPT: 79 – rheumatoid arthritis with rheumatoid factor of multiple sites without organ or systems involvement.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_02084_76011_p4]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Evaluation of an automated phenotyping algorithm for rheumatoid arthritis.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Using data from 2015–2018, including 34 million notes from 854,628 patients, 158 practices and 24 EHRs, the researchers developed and evaluated a natural language processing (NLP) pipeline for extracting mentions of rheumatoid arthritis outcome measures and scores from free-text outpatient rheumatology notes within the Rheumatology Informatics System for Effectiveness (RISE) registry.", "allowed": {"1": ["shard_00350_78120_p1", "shard_04604_9387_p19", "shard_06100_7595_p2"], "2": ["shard_03655_15858_p3", "shard_04604_9387_p18", "shard_04604_9387_p19"], "3": ["shard_01004_15456_p1", "shard_02858_24324_p10", "shard_04604_9387_p19"], "4": ["shard_00326_34840_p36", "shard_01004_15456_p1", "shard_02084_76011_p4"]}, "raw": "{\"patches\":[]}", "stats": {"proposed": 0, "accepted": 0, "rejected": 0}, "errors": [], "original": "Your professor probably means: turn free-text clues in de-identified Electronic Health Records, or EHRs, into stan