{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T231601956950+1000.conduct_an_analysis_to_determine.output.json", "system": "You are the final claim-completion editor for a cited research draft. You\nreceive numbered draft lines and at most four material findings, each paired\nwith citation-local source excerpts. Return JSON only:\n{\"patches\":[{\"finding\":1,\"mode\":\"replace\",\"line\":12,\"sentence\":\"One complete sentence. [unit_id]\"}]}\n\nFor each finding, either replace one related draft line or insert one sentence\nafter a related line, or add citation markers without changing its prose. Use\n`mode` equal to `replace`, `insert_after`, or `cite`.\n\nRules:\n- Address only the numbered findings using only their ALLOWED CITATION ids.\n- Prefer `replace` when a draft line already begins the point but lacks its\n  exact name, value, comparison, population, time/scope, source, or limitation.\n- A replacement must preserve the original line's point and citations while\n  completing it; an insertion must add one genuinely absent atomic point.\n- Use `cite` for an otherwise sound uncited medical, safety, or causal\n  synthesis. Its sentence words must remain exactly unchanged; append only\n  directly supporting allowed citations.\n- For a requested measurement, state its value, population/sample, time/scope,\n  and source or study name when the excerpts support them. A nearby generic\n  study is not a substitute for the finding's subject-outcome combination.\n- For a named safety, legal, or penalty obligation, state the exact name and\n  how it constrains this request rather than offering generic caution.\n- Every sentence must be one line, at most 55 words, and end with at least one\n  allowed citation. Use each finding at most once and return at most four\n  patches.\n- Do not add headings, lists, sections, commentary, or an entire worked\n  example. Do not rewrite, delete, merge, split, or reorder any other line.\n- Omit a finding when its evidence does not directly support a compact patch.\n  An empty patches array is successful.\n", "packet": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.\n\nNUMBERED ACCEPTED DRAFT — PRESERVE UNPATCHED LINES\n1: Social media means platforms that let users create, distribute and algorithmically discover content and relationships; this assessment covers the mass-platform era from roughly 2004 to 6 August 2026, across the regions represented in the evidence, and compares it with a society retaining earlier websites, messaging, broadcasting and offline networks but lacking engagement-ranked mass feeds. \n2: “Beneficial” means a durable, broadly distributed improvement in welfare, knowledge, agency, opportunity or rights, while “detrimental” means the reverse; the balance considers magnitude, population reach, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution and causal confidence rather than merely counting effects. \n3: Causal confidence is strongest when random assignment or a natural experiment approximates the missing counterfactual, whereas surveys and cross-sectional associations remain vulnerable to self-selection and reverse causality, including the possibility that distressed people select more social media or polarized societies generate polarized feeds. [shard_03588_70930] [shard_01051_58704] [shard_03406_3576]\n4: In information, democracy and public safety, the beneficial side includes rapid news access, political expression, participation, collective action, citizen documentation and emergency awareness: a 499-article academic review found the clearest gain in participation, while case studies record ordinary people exposing Abu Ghraib abuse, Iranian election violence and disasters, Myanmar journalists connecting citizens with officials and NGOs, and Facebook and Twitter supporting crisis communication. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_01867_61565] [shard_02059_76178]\n5: The detrimental side is larger and more durable because misinformation, harassment, institutional distrust, homophily and extremism scale cheaply, with academic work associating social media with detrimental changes in trust and all five negative indicators—hate, polarization, populism, network homophily and misinformation—and reports that, beginning in 2013, Myanmar military members were believed to have used fake Facebook identities for incendiary anti-Rohingya posts where Facebook was many people’s main or only route to online news and internet use. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_01568_51801] [shard_00992_13016]\n6: Myanmar also exposes unequal winners and losers: in a survey of 1,340 young people concerning first-time voters’ political-news consumption, 80% reported social-movement involvement, yet Facebook had only two Burmese-speaking harmful-content reviewers for roughly 50 million people in early 2015, and Lu Nge Khit later reported that posts about conflict, torture, arson and killings were often removed. [shard_01247_18777] [shard_00992_13016] [shard_00569_38420]\n7: The evidence nevertheless counsels against monocausal claims: Russian troll material was estimated at only 0.004% of U.S. Facebook-feed content, and studies of Facebook’s and Instagram’s recommendation algorithms around the 2020 U.S. election found increased engagement and time online but no detectable change in existing political attitudes or polarization, even as the algorithms increased exposure to uncivil content and reduced political content from moderate friends and ideologically mixed audiences. [shard_06261_13612] [shard_03177_74042]\n8: In individual and relational well-being, benefits are genuine for geographically separated families, shy or marginalized young people, identity exploration, creative communities and pandemic-era support, while body-positive and recovery networks can improve mood and body appreciation; these gains concentrate among people whose offline alternatives are weakest. [shard_00741_18887] [shard_01581_66087] [shard_02027_72940]\n9: Harms include compulsive attention, comparison, anxiety, depression, loneliness, displaced face-to-face intimacy and body pressure, with a 50-study review across 17 countries identifying girls and people already concerned about weight as particularly vulnerable and a cross-sectional pilot survey of 700 northeastern U.S. middle-school students—52% of them from racial or ethnic minorities—finding social-media-related body dissatisfaction in 19% of respondents. [shard_03023_52901] [shard_01350_54628] [shard_00741_18887]\n10: Causal studies strengthen but circumscribe that conclusion: a three-week randomized limit of no more than 30 minutes total per day across Facebook, Instagram and Snapchat reduced depression and loneliness among 143 Penn students but produced null effects on support, self-esteem and overall well-being, while Facebook’s staggered introduction across 775 colleges—a natural experiment—was associated with 7% more severe functional depression and 20% more anxiety disorders, especially among socially isolated or indebted students. [shard_03588_70930] [shard_01051_58704]\n11: Thus active, purposeful use can produce immediate and reversible connection, whereas passive comparison and recommender-driven repetition can accumulate into longer-lived harms, with adolescents, poorer users and people already depressed, isolated or body-conscious facing the least favorable balance and the weakest ability to opt out. \n12: In economic opportunity, institutions and rights, benefits include inexpensive marketing, crowdfunding, professional networking, hiring, entrepreneurship, education and health communication: studies describe global knowledge access and collaboration, Australian small-business adoption, and MENA surveys in which 86% viewed social media as important to start-ups and 71% expected to use it for their next job. [shard_03385_45918] [shard_04212_39856] [shard_06366_43784]\n13: Named health cases show Facebook subsidizing trusted vaccine campaigns, WhatsApp and chatbots connecting authorities with publics, Japanese platforms supporting COVID fact-checking, and Twitter patients coining and spreading “long COVID,” although a 31-study WHO review also found that medical misinformation increased fear, misunderstanding and barriers to care. [shard_01618_10932] [shard_01949_73119]\n14: Against those gains stand surveillance, scams, concentrated gatekeeping and hidden labor: Cambridge Analytica harvested millions of Facebook profiles without consent, U.S. consumers reported $770 million in social-media-originated fraud losses in 2021, and Kenyan Facebook contractors alleged PTSD after inadequately supported exposure to graphic moderation work. [shard_01677_30821] [shard_03319_35805] [shard_02380_17754]\n15: Platform dependence also redistributes power from creators and institutions to opaque ranking systems, as seven Indian journalism start-ups found that engagement economics discouraged minority coverage and TikTok was reported to disadvantage minority, disabled and overweight creators, turning nominally equal access into unequal visibility and income. [shard_06536_4631] [shard_03462_20779]\n16: The mechanism connecting all three domains is incentive design: ranking individual posts by engagement can give uniformly high scores to controversial material, while recommender-system manipulation can shape users’ views without their knowledge and extensive data collection creates additional security risks. [shard_03914_79668] [shard_03147_70901]\n17: Governance can change that balance: Section 230 broadly protects U.S. online platforms from liability for user-generated content, generating partisan disputes over both viewpoint censorship and inadequate action against misinformation, whereas the European Union has opened an investigation into TikTok’s compliance with the Digital Services Act. [shard_00913_73926] [shard_06113_43175] [shard_00804_29144]\n18: Overall, social media’s impact in the engagement-optimized mass-platform era has been more detrimental than beneficial: its connection, knowledge and opportunity gains are substantial but often substitutable, targetable and reversible, whereas violence, youth developmental injury, privacy loss and institutional distrust are more unevenly imposed, persistent and sometimes irreversible. \n19: Information, democracy and public safety receives the greatest weight because it has the broadest spillovers—corrupted information weakens health, markets, relationships and regulation simultaneously—and Myanmar demonstrates that platform failure can convert communication advantages into physical persecution, making this domain’s severe downside outweigh the other domains’ net benefits. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_00992_13016] [shard_05518_79195]\n\nMATERIAL FINDINGS WITH CITATION-LOCAL EVIDENCE\nFINDING 1\nREQUIREMENT: Attach citations to population-level health, safety, and causal conclusions.\nDRAFT GAP: The uncited well-being synthesis beginning “Thus active, purposeful use...” generalizes causal mental-health effects and vulnerable populations. The uncited overall verdict also asserts violence and youth developmental injury as persistent or irreversible outcomes.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_01350_54628_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Their social media use is also at its peak.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: \"Despite the expansive literature on mainstream TV's effects on young people's body image, evidence is only emerging on social media's role in influencing healthy body image,\" said Charmaraman, who leads the Youth, Media & Wellbeing Research Lab at the Wellesley Centers for Women. \"I am thankful to my undergraduate research assistants who advocated for the significance of this issue and helped shape the design of the study.\"\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_01812_67769_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Discussions on the link between social media use and mental health are nothing new, but researchers at the University of Pennsylvania for the first time conducted a study based on experimental data that connects the causal relationship between social media use and mental well-being.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: What they found was that simply limiting social media use could be beneficial when it comes to better mental health, specifically when it comes to depression and loneliness.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_01247_18777_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: The study also shows that youth have great interest in quality reporting such as investigative journalism and in topics that are specifically youth oriented.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Only one percent of respondents do not use Facebook.\n\nFINDING 2\nREQUIREMENT: In every domain, provide at least two academic studies, two journalistic reports, and two named platform case studies spanning two regions.\nDRAFT GAP: The individual and relational well-being domain does not concretely identify two journalistic reports or two named platform case studies across two regions. The information/democracy domain also does not clearly identify two named platform case studies spanning two regions.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_06536_4631_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: An interdisciplinary research agenda that combines approaches from platform studies and (journalistic) production studies is advocated in this paper.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: However, they do not adequately engage with platform power.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_03177_74042_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Perhaps not — at least, not according to some of the peer-reviewed studies, released late July, that resulted from a collaboration between Facebook parent company Meta and outside researchers.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: There are, however, issues with the research design in some of these studies that merit discussion.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_03588_70930_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Positive gains were also found in anxiety as well as the fear of \"missing out.\" The group that was limited, however, did not show any change regarding feelings of social support, self-esteem, or psychological well-being.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: The study is not without its drawbacks.\n\nFINDING 3\nREQUIREMENT: Within every domain, contrast short- and long-run effects, winners and losers, platform design and user behavior, and age, socioeconomic, and vulnerability differences.\nDRAFT GAP: The information domain lacks a socioeconomic disaggregation and a concrete short-versus-long-run comparison. The economic/institutions/rights domain lacks age and socioeconomic disaggregation and a short-versus-long-run comparison.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_03177_74042_p2]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: In contrast to observational data like surveys that are based on what one declares, behavioral data are collected by tracking user activity and represent actual behavior.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: This research design, in which behavioral data is benchmarked against observational data collected over a short period of time, seems to have been repeated in three out of the four studies released in Science and Nature.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_03147_70901_p2]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Question: How can recommendation systems influence user behavior and perception?\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Answer: By lengthening exposure to similar content, they can affect behavior, alter perception, and influence election results.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_02418_70967_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: The idea that internet platforms are just conduits in the information age is withering away.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: The idea that internet platforms like Facebook and YouTube are mere conduits in the information age used to be the motif of the traditional legal apparatus.\n\nFINDING 4\nREQUIREMENT: Compare the Digital Services Act and Section 230 through one concrete content-regulation or platform-liability example and explain the resulting incentive and net-impact difference.\nDRAFT GAP: The draft juxtaposes a general description of Section 230 with an EU TikTok investigation, but does not apply both regimes to one concrete example or show how their differing rules change platform incentives and the net judgment.\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_00913_73926_p2]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: And Section 230 was born.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Page 2 of document: How Section 230 helped shape speech on the Internet\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_06113_43175_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: With the reintroduction of Section 230 reform bills, such as the SAFE TECH Act, and recent congressional hearings on platform accountability, there is mounting pressure on congressional committee chairs to find common ground and reform the statute.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: These decisions indicate that Section 230 remains unchanged for now and that internet platforms are not at risk of facing a deluge of lawsuits if they fail to remove content that could be labeled as \"terrorist.\"\nALLOWED CITATION: [shard_02418_70967_p1]\nSOURCE EXCERPT: Section 230 of the CDA stipulates that those who provide an “interactive computer service” are exempt from liability for third party content that they publish.\nSOURCE EXCERPT: The original Communications Decency Act of 1996 did not last long.", "allowed": {"1": ["shard_01247_18777_p1", "shard_01350_54628_p1", "shard_01812_67769_p1"], "2": ["shard_03177_74042_p1", "shard_03588_70930_p1", "shard_06536_4631_p1"], "3": ["shard_02418_70967_p1", "shard_03147_70901_p2", "shard_03177_74042_p2"], "4": ["shard_00913_73926_p2", "shard_02418_70967_p1", "shard_06113_43175_p1"]}, "raw": "{\"patches\":[{\"finding\":1,\"mode\":\"replace\",\"line\":11,\"sentence\":\"Thus causal evidence supports an immediate, potentially reversible mental-health benefit from limiting use—lower depression and loneliness—while evidence on social media’s influence on healthy body image remains emergent, making broader claims about lasting harms or vulnerability differences less certain. [shard_01812_67769_p1] [shard_01350_54628_p1]\"},{\"finding\":3,\"mode\":\"insert_after\",\"line\":16,\"sentence\":\"Short-period behavioral-versus-observational benchmarks may not capture longer-run design effects because recommendation systems can lengthen exposure to similar content and thereby alter behavior and perception. [shard_03177_74042_p2] [shard_03147_70901_p2]\"}]}", "stats": {"proposed": 2, "accepted": 0, "rejected": 2}, "errors": ["a replacement did not preserve its claim", "a claim patch would exceed the report word cap"], "original": "Social media means platforms that let users create, distribute and algorithmically discover content and relationships; this assessment covers the mass-platform era from roughly 2004 to 6 August 2026, across the regions represented in the evidence, and compares it with a society retaining earlier websites, messaging, broadcasting and offline networks but lacking engagement-ranked mass feeds. \n“Beneficial” means a durable, broadly distributed improvement in welfare, knowledge, agency, opportunity or rights, while “detrimental” means the reverse; the balance considers magnitude, population reach, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution and causal confidence rather than merely counting effects. \nCausal confidence is strongest when random assignment or a natural experiment approximates the missing counterfactual, whereas surveys and cross-sectional associations remain vulnerable to self-selection and reverse causality, including the possibility that distressed people select more social media or polarized societies generate polarized feeds. [shard_03588_70930] [shard_01051_58704] [shard_03406_3576]\nIn information, democracy and public safety, the beneficial side includes rapid news access, political expression, participation, collective action, citizen documentation and emergency awareness: a 499-article academic review found the clearest gain in participation, while case studies record ordinary people exposing Abu Ghraib abuse, Iranian election violence and disasters, Myanmar journalists connecting citizens with officials and NGOs, and Facebook and Twitter supporting crisis communication. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_01867_61565] [shard_02059_76178]\nThe detrimental side is larger and more durable because misinformation, harassment, institutional distrust, homophily and extremism scale cheaply, with academic work associating social media with detrimental changes in trust and all five negative indicators—hate, polarization, populism, network homophily and misinformation—and reports that, beginning in 2013, Myanmar military members were believed to have used fake Facebook identities for incendiary anti-Rohingya posts where Facebook was many people’s main or only route to online news and internet use. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_01568_51801] [shard_00992_13016]\nMyanmar also exposes unequal winners and losers: in a survey of 1,340 young people concerning first-time voters’ political-news consumption, 80% reported social-movement involvement, yet Facebook had only two Burmese-speaking harmful-content reviewers for roughly 50 million people in early 2015, and Lu Nge Khit later reported that posts about conflict, torture, arson and killings were often removed. [shard_01247_18777] [shard_00992_13016] [shard_00569_38420]\nThe evidence nevertheless counsels against monocausal claims: Russian troll material was estimated at only 0.004% of U.S. Facebook-feed content, and studies of Facebook’s and Instagram’s recommendation algorithms around the 2020 U.S. election found increased engagement and time online but no detectable change in existing political attitudes or polarization, even as the algorithms increased exposure to uncivil content and reduced political content from moderate friends and ideologically mixed audiences. [shard_0626