 missing counterfactual, whereas surveys and cross-sectional associations remain vulnerable to self-selection and reverse causality, including the possibility that distressed people select more social media or polarized societies generate polarized feeds. [shard_03588_70930] [shard_01051_58704] [shard_03406_3576]\nIn information, democracy and public safety, the beneficial side includes rapid news access, political expression, participation, collective action, citizen documentation and emergency awareness: a 499-article academic review found the clearest gain in participation, while case studies record ordinary people exposing Abu Ghraib abuse, Iranian election violence and disasters, Myanmar journalists connecting citizens with officials and NGOs, and Facebook and Twitter supporting crisis communication. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_01867_61565] [shard_02059_76178]\nThe detrimental side is larger and more durable because misinformation, harassment, institutional distrust, homophily and extremism scale cheaply, with academic work associating social media with detrimental changes in trust and all five negative indicators—hate, polarization, populism, network homophily and misinformation—and reports that, beginning in 2013, Myanmar military members were believed to have used fake Facebook identities for incendiary anti-Rohingya posts where Facebook was many people’s main or only route to online news and internet use. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_01568_51801] [shard_00992_13016]\nMyanmar also exposes unequal winners and losers: in a survey of 1,340 young people concerning first-time voters’ political-news consumption, 80% reported social-movement involvement, yet Facebook had only two Burmese-speaking harmful-content reviewers for roughly 50 million people in early 2015, and Lu Nge Khit later reported that posts about conflict, torture, arson and killings were often removed. [shard_01247_18777] [shard_00992_13016] [shard_00569_38420]\nThe evidence nevertheless counsels against monocausal claims: Russian troll material was estimated at only 0.004% of U.S. Facebook-feed content, and studies of Facebook’s and Instagram’s recommendation algorithms around the 2020 U.S. election found increased engagement and time online but no detectable change in existing political attitudes or polarization, even as the algorithms increased exposure to uncivil content and reduced political content from moderate friends and ideologically mixed audiences. [shard_06261_13612] [shard_03177_74042]\nIn individual and relational well-being, benefits are genuine for geographically separated families, shy or marginalized young people, identity exploration, creative communities and pandemic-era support, while body-positive and recovery networks can improve mood and body appreciation; these gains concentrate among people whose offline alternatives are weakest. [shard_00741_18887] [shard_01581_66087] [shard_02027_72940]\nHarms include compulsive attention, comparison, anxiety, depression, loneliness, displaced face-to-face intimacy and body pressure, with a 50-study review across 17 countries identifying girls and people already concerned about weight as particularly vulnerable and a cross-sectional pilot survey of 700 northeastern U.S. middle-school students—52% of them from racial or ethnic minorities—finding social-media-related body dissatisfaction in 19% of respondents. [shard_03023_52901] [shard_01350_54628] [shard_00741_18887]\nCausal studies strengthen but circumscribe that conclusion: a three-week randomized limit of no more than 30 minutes total per day across Facebook, Instagram and Snapchat reduced depression and loneliness among 143 Penn students but produced null effects on support, self-esteem and overall well-being, while Facebook’s staggered introduction across 775 colleges—a natural experiment—was associated with 7% more severe functional depression and 20% more anxiety disorders, especially among socially isolated or indebted students. [shard_03588_70930] [shard_01051_58704]\nThus active, purposeful use can produce immediate and reversible connection, whereas passive comparison and recommender-driven repetition can accumulate into longer-lived harms, with adolescents, poorer users and people already depressed, isolated or body-conscious facing the least favorable balance and the weakest ability to opt out. \nIn economic opportunity, institutions and rights, benefits include inexpensive marketing, crowdfunding, professional networking, hiring, entrepreneurship, education and health communication: studies describe global knowledge access and collaboration, Australian small-business adoption, and MENA surveys in which 86% viewed social media as important to start-ups and 71% expected to use it for their next job. [shard_03385_45918] [shard_04212_39856] [shard_06366_43784]\nNamed health cases show Facebook subsidizing trusted vaccine campaigns, WhatsApp and chatbots connecting authorities with publics, Japanese platforms supporting COVID fact-checking, and Twitter patients coining and spreading “long COVID,” although a 31-study WHO review also found that medical misinformation increased fear, misunderstanding and barriers to care. [shard_01618_10932] [shard_01949_73119]\nAgainst those gains stand surveillance, scams, concentrated gatekeeping and hidden labor: Cambridge Analytica harvested millions of Facebook profiles without consent, U.S. consumers reported $770 million in social-media-originated fraud losses in 2021, and Kenyan Facebook contractors alleged PTSD after inadequately supported exposure to graphic moderation work. [shard_01677_30821] [shard_03319_35805] [shard_02380_17754]\nPlatform dependence also redistributes power from creators and institutions to opaque ranking systems, as seven Indian journalism start-ups found that engagement economics discouraged minority coverage and TikTok was reported to disadvantage minority, disabled and overweight creators, turning nominally equal access into unequal visibility and income. [shard_06536_4631] [shard_03462_20779]\nThe mechanism connecting all three domains is incentive design: ranking individual posts by engagement can give uniformly high scores to controversial material, while recommender-system manipulation can shape users’ views without their knowledge and extensive data collection creates additional security risks. [shard_03914_79668] [shard_03147_70901]\nGovernance can change that balance: Section 230 broadly protects U.S. online platforms from liability for user-generated content, generating partisan disputes over both viewpoint censorship and inadequate action against misinformation, whereas the European Union has opened an investigation into TikTok’s compliance with the Digital Services Act. [shard_00913_73926] [shard_06113_43175] [shard_00804_29144]\nOverall, social media’s impact in the engagement-optimized mass-platform era has been more detrimental than beneficial: its connection, knowledge and opportunity gains are substantial but often substitutable, targetable and reversible, whereas violence, youth developmental injury, privacy loss and institutional distrust are more unevenly imposed, persistent and sometimes irreversible. \nInformation, democracy and public safety receives the greatest weight because it has the broadest spillovers—corrupted information weakens health, markets, relationships and regulation simultaneously—and Myanmar demonstrates that platform failure can convert communication advantages into physical persecution, making this domain’s severe downside outweigh the other domains’ net benefits. [shard_03406_3576] [shard_00992_13016] [shard_05518_79195]", "documents": 40, "usage": {"input_tokens": 3569, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 823}}
